//! LLVM modules built as records, their fragments, and their encoding as bitcode.
//!
//! # Details
//! A `Module` holds what the bitcode records hold: interned types and constants, attribute lists,
//! globals unified by name, and function bodies as a stream of tagged words. The backend builds
//! one module per instance definition through the instruction emitters here, and stores it as a
//! fragment: a self-delimiting byte string, so fragments concatenate wherever IR used to. A codegen
//! unit reads its fragments back, merges them into one module, remapping types, constants,
//! attribute lists and globals, and encodes that as bitcode.
//!
//! A body word's low four bits tag it. A record starts with a header word holding its code from bit
//! 8 and, in bit 4, whether it defines a value; each operand holds its payload from bit 4: a plain
//! literal, a value reference written relative, typed, absolute or signed, a type id, an attribute
//! list or a block. Type ids, attribute lists and value references are remapped when fragments
//! merge, and blocks are numbered per body.
//!
//! A value reference names a local value of the body being built, an interned constant or a global
//! in its low two bits. Locals are the arguments then each value-producing instruction, in order. A
//! value used before it is defined, such as a `phi`'s incoming value from a later block, is
//! `reserve`d and `bind`s to the real value later; a block is a handle from `newBlock` whose
//! position is the order of `startBlock` calls. Both resolve when the body ends.
//!
//! Names only serve the printed text: bitcode carries no value names. A name packs a prefix id with
//! up to two numbers (`v12`, `carg3.1`), and zero leaves a value unnamed.
//!
//! The encoding follows LLVM 22's `BitcodeWriter`, without metadata, value symbol tables or
//! abbreviations other than the string table's blob: the reader takes names from the string table
//! and function bodies in the order of their declarations.

